d an odd function.\n\n3. **Integral Property of Odd Functions:**\n   For any odd function \\(f(x)\\), the integral over symmetric limits around zero is zero. Formally:\n   \\[\n   \\int_{-a}^{a} f(x) \\, dx = 0\n   \\]\n   Here, \\([-1, 1]\\) is a symmetric interval around zero.\n\n4. **Evaluating the Integral:**\n   Applying the property of odd functions:\n   \\[\n   \\int_{-1}^{1} (3^x + 3^{-x}) \\tan{x} \\, dx = 0\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   The value of the integral is \\(0\\).\n\n### Final Answer:\n\\(\\boxed{0}\\)"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the integral \\(\\int_{-1}^{1} (3^x+3^{-x}) \\tan{x} \\, dx.\\) Determine if the integrand is an odd function and use this information to find the value of the integral.\nassistant\n", "response": "1. **Analyze the integrand:**\n\nThe given integral is \\(\\int_{-1}^{1} (3^x + 3^{-x}) \\tan{x} \\, dx.\\)\n\nWe see that the function \\(3^x + 3^{-x}\\) is an even function because \\(3^{-x} + 3^x = 3^x + 3^{-x}\\). This means that it is symmetric about the \\(y\\)-axis.\n\nThe function \\(\\tan{x}\\), on the other hand, is an odd function, as \\(\\tan{(-x)} = -\\tan{x}\\). This means that it is symmetric with respect to the origin.\n\n2. **Product of an even and odd function:**\n\nSince the integrand is the product of an even function and an odd function, the resulting function will be odd. \n\n**Reason:**  \n- Even: \\((3^x + 3^{-x}) = (3^{-x} + 3^x)\\)\n- Odd: \\(\\tan{-x} = -\\tan{x}\\)\n- Odd: \\((3^x + 3^{-x}) \\tan{x} = -\\left((3^{-x} + 3^x) \\tan{-x}\\right) = -\\left((3^{-x} + 3^x) -\\tan{x}\\right) = -\\left((3^x + 3^{-x}) \\tan{x}\\right)\\) \n\n3. **Integral over an odd function:**\n\nRecall that the integral of an odd function over an interval symmetric about the origin is zero:\n\n\\[\n\\int_{-a}^{a} f(x) \\, dx = 0\n\\]\n\nwhen \\(f(x) = -f(-x)\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n\nSince 