 is 4).\n\\[ \\text{Average} = \\frac{20}{4} = 5 \\]\n\nThus, the smallest possible average of four distinct positive even integers is \\(\\boxed{5}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Find the limit of the expression \\( \\frac{e^{x}+x-\\cos(2x)}{x^2} \\) as \\( x \\to \\infty \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{\\infty}."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Find the limit of the expression \\( \\frac{e^{x}+x-\\cos(2x)}{x^2} \\) as \\( x \\to \\infty \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{L}. \n\nTo solve this, we consider the limit of each individual term in the expression as \\( x \\to \\infty \\).\n\n- \\(\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} e^x = \\infty \\)\n- \\(\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} x = \\infty \\)\n- \\(\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\cos(2x) = \\text{undefined} \\) (Since the cosine function oscillates between -1 and 1, it does not approach a single value as \\( x \\to \\infty \\))\n- \\(\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} x^2 = \\infty \\)\n\nGiven that \\( e^x \\) dominates all other terms as \\( x \\to \\infty \\), we can focus on the limit of \\(\\frac{e^x}{x^2}\\) as \\( x \\to \\infty \\).\n\nWe apply L'Hôpital's rule twice on \\(\\frac{e^x}{x^2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\frac{e^x}{x^2} = \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\frac{e^x}{2x} = \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\frac{e^x}{2} = \\infty\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{\\infty}."}]
