l answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Let $H$ be a real Hilbert space and $C$ be a closed convex subset of $H$. If $x$ is a boundary point of $C$, does there exist a point $y \\in H \\setminus C$ such that the half-line $\\{x + t(y - x); t > 0\\}$ lies entirely in $H \\setminus C$? Answer yes or no.\nassistant\n", "response": "We start by looking at $C^\\perp$ and $\\mathrm{Proj}(x) = y$. Since there is a unique way of writing $x = u+v$ with $u \\in C$ and $v \\in C^\\perp$ we have $y = u$. Since $C$ is closed and convex this implies $u \\in C$. For $0 \\leq t \\leq 1$ we then find that $z_t = tx + (1-t)y = tv +(1-t)u \\in C$. Therefore we cannot have $x \\in \\partial C$. This implies the answer is $\\boxed{\\text{no}}$."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Let $H$ be a real Hilbert space and $C$ be a closed convex subset of $H$. If $x$ is a boundary point of $C$, does there exist a point $y \\in H \\setminus C$ such that the half-line $\\{x + t(y - x); t > 0\\}$ lies entirely in $H \\setminus C$? Answer yes or no.\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}.$"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Let $H$ be a real Hilbert space and $C$ be a closed convex subset of $H$. If $x$ is a boundary point of $C$, does there exist a point $y \\in H \\setminus C$ such that the half-line $\\{x + t(y - x); t > 0\\}$ lies entirely in $H \\setminus C$? Answer yes or no.\nassistant\n", "response": "Let $H$ be a real Hilbert space and $C$ be a closed convex subset of $H$. Suppose $x$ is a boundary point of $C$. We want to determine if there exists a point $y \\in H \\setminus C$ such that the half-line $\\{x + t(y - x); t > 0\\}$ lies entirely in $H \\setminus C$.\n\nBy the Separation Theorem for convex sets in Hilbert spaces, there exists a linear functional $f \\in H^*$ and a real number $c$ such that\n$$\\langle f, c \\rangle \\leq c < \\langle f, y \\rangle$$\nfor all $c \\in C$ and $y \\in H \\setminus C$. Since $x$ is a boundary point of $C$, there exists a sequence $\\{c_n\\} \\subseteq C$ converging to $x$. Therefore, we have\n$$\\langle f, x \\rangle = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\langle f, c_n \\rangle \\leq c.$$\n\nNow, let $y \\in H \\setminus C$. Then, we have\n$$\\langle f, x + t(y - x) \\rangle = (1 - t) \\langle f, x \\rangle + t \\langle f, y \\rangle > c$$\nfor all $t > 0$. This implies that $x + t(y - x) \\in H \\setminus C$ for all $t > 0$. Thus, the half-line $\\{x + t(y - x); t > 0\\}$ lies entirely in $H \\setminus C$.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Let $H$ be a real Hilbert space and $C$ be a closed convex subset of $H$. If $x$ is a boundary point of $C$, does there exist a point $y \\in H \\setminus C$ such that the half-line $\\{x + t(y - x); t > 0\\}$ lies entirely in $H \\setminus C$? Answer yes or no.\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}$.\n\nIn fact, since \\( x \\) is a boundary point of \\( C \\), there exists a sequence \\(\\{y_n\\} \\subset H \\setminus C\\) such that \\(y_n \\to x\\) as \\(n \\to \\infty\\). We can choose a particular \\(y = y_N\\) for some \\(N\\) such that the half-line \\(\\{x + t(y - x); t > 0\\}\\) lies entirely in \\(H \\setminus C\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Let $H$ be a real Hilbert space and $C$ be a closed convex subset of $H$. If $x$ is a boundary point of $C$, does there exist a point $y \\in H \\setminus C$ such that the half-line $\\{x + t(y - x); t > 0\\}$ lies entirely in $H \\setminus C$? Answer yes or no.\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}$. The answer is trivially $\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}$. For a closed convex set $C$, every boundary point $x$ of $C$ is also a boundary point of $H \\setminus C$. There exists a point $y \\in H \\setminus C$ such that the half-line $\\{x + t(y - x); t > 0\\}$ lies entirely in $H \\setminus C$.\n\nFor example, consider $C = \\{x \\in H : \\|x\\| \\leq 1\\}$. If $x = e_1$ is a boundary point of $C$, then we can choose $y = 2e_1 \\in H \\setminus C$ and the half-line $\\{x + t(y - x); t > 0\\} = \\{e_1 + t(e_1 - e_1); t > 0\\} = \\{2e_1 + t(e_1 - e_1); t > 0\\} = \\{2e_1 + te_1 - te_1; t > 0\\} = \\{2e_1; t > 0\\}$ lies entirely in $H \\setminus C$.\n\nSimilarly, if we consider $C = \\{x \\in H : \\|x\\| \\leq 1\\}$, $x = 0$ and $y = e_1 \\in H \\setminus C$, we can see that the half-line $\\{x + t(y - x); t > 0\\} = \\{0 + t(e_1 - 0); t > 0\\} = \\{te_1; t > 0\\}$ lies entirely in $H \\setminus C$.\n\nThus, we can conclude that for a closed convex set $C$, every boundary point $x$ of $C$ is also a boundary point of $H \\setminus C$. There exists a point $y \\in H \\setminus C$ such that the half-line $\\{x + t(y - x); t > 0\\}$ lies entirely in $H \\setminus C$.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}$."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Is it always true that for every non-trivial multivariate polynomial \\( F \\in \\mathbb{Z}[x_1, \\dots, x_n] \\), there exist infinitely many tuples \\( (a_1, \\dots, a_n) \\in \\mathbb{N}^n \\) such that \\( F(a) \\neq 0 \\)?\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}$."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Is it always true that for every non-trivial multivariate polynomial \\( F \\in \\mathbb{Z}[x_1, \\dots, x_n] \\), there exist infinitely many tuples \\( (a_1, \\dots, a_n) \\in \\mathbb{N}^