on \\( f(z) = e^z \\) is analytic everywhere, and the contour is the circle \\( |z|=1 \\). We need to find the value of the integral \\( \\oint_{|z|=1}\\frac{e^z}{z}\\,\\mathrm dz \\). Here, \\( f(z) = e^z \\) and \\( a = 0 \\).\n\nAccording to the Cauchy Integral Formula,\n\n\\[\n\\oint_{|z|=1}\\frac{e^z}{z}\\,\\mathrm dz = 2\\pi i e^0 = 2\\pi i \\cdot 1 = 2\\pi i.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the integral is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\pi i}.\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the complex line integral \\( \\oint_{|z|=1}\\frac{e^z}{z}\\,\\mathrm dz \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the complex line integral \\( \\oint_{|z|=1} \\frac{e^z}{z} \\, \\mathrm{d}z \\), we will use the Cauchy Integral Formula. The Cauchy Integral Formula states that for a function \\( f(z) \\) that is analytic inside and on a simple closed contour \\( C \\), and for any point \\( a \\) inside \\( C \\),\n\n\\[\nf(a) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i} \\oint_C \\frac{f(z)}{z-a} \\, \\mathrm{d}z.\n\\]\n\nIn our case, the function \\( f(z) = e^z \\) is analytic everywhere, and we are integrating \\( \\frac{e^z}{z} \\) around the unit circle \\( |z| = 1 \\). Here, \\( a = 0 \\), so we can apply the Cauchy Integral Formula directly:\n\n\\[\n\\oint_{|z|=1} \\frac{e^z}{z} \\, \\mathrm{d}z = 2\\pi i f(0).\n\\]\n\nSince \\( f(z) = e^z \\), we have \\( f(0) = e^0 = 1 \\). Therefore,\n\n\\[\n\\oint_{|z|=1} \\frac{e^z}{z} \\, \\mathrm{d}z = 2\\pi i \\cdot 1 = 2\\pi i.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of the complex line integral is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi i}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the surface integral \\(\\int_S \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{dS}\\) where \\(\\mathbf{F}=\\langle 7x,2y,5z\\rangle\\) and \\(S\\) is the boundary surface of a region \\(V\\) with unit volume.\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the surface integral \\(\\int_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{dS}\\) where \\(\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 7x, 2y, 5z \\rangle\\) and \\(S\\) is the boundary surface of a region \\(V\\) with unit volume, we can use the Divergence Theorem. The Divergence Theorem states that\n\n\\[\n\\int_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{dS} = \\int_V \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F} \\, dV.\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to compute the divergence of the vector field \\(\\mathbf{F}\\). The divergence of \\(\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 7x, 2y, 5z \\rangle\\) is given by\n\n\\[\n\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(7x) + \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(2y) + \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}(5z) = 7 + 2 + 5 = 14.\n\\]\n\nSince the divergence of \\(\\mathbf{F}\\) is a constant, the integral becomes\n\n\\[\n\\int_V \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F} \\, dV = \\int_V 14 \\, dV = 14 \\int_V dV.\n\\]\n\nThe integral \\(\\int_V dV\\) is the volume of the region \\(V\\), which is given as 1. Therefore,\n\n\\[\n\\int_V 14 \\, dV = 14 \\cdot 1 = 14.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of the surface integral is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}.\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the surface integral \\(\\int_S \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{dS}\\) where \\(\\mathbf{F}=\\langle 7x,2y,5z\\rangle\\) and \\(S\\) is the boundary surface of a region \\(V\\) with unit volume.\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the surface integral \\(\\int_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{dS}\\) where \\(\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 7x, 2y, 5z \\rangle\\) and \\(S\\) is the boundary surface of a region \\(V\\) with unit volume, we can use the Divergence Theorem.\n\nThe Divergence Theorem states that for a vector field \\(\\mathbf{F}\\) and a region \\(V\\) with a boundary surface \\(S\\),\n\\[\n\\int_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{dS} = \\iiint_V \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F} \\, dV,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F}\\) is the divergence of the vector field \\(\\mathbf{F}\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the divergence of \\(\\mathbf{F}\\):\n\\[\n\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(7x) + \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(2y) + \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}(5z) = 7 + 2 + 5 = 14.\n\\]\nSo, the divergence of \\(\\mathbf{F}\\) is 14.\n\nUsing the Divergence Theorem, we have\n\\[\n\\int_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{dS} = \\iiint_V 14 \\, dV = 14 \\iiint_V dV = 14 \\text{ (since the volume of } V \\text{ is 1)}.\n\\]\nTherefore, the value of the surface integral is \\(\\boxed{14}\\)."}]
