the value of \\( x \\) for which \\( |3x+5| \\) is not positive is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac{5}{3}}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. A recipe calls for $3\\frac{4}{5}$ cups of flour. However, Stacy wants to use half as much flour as the recipe calls for. How many cups of flour will Stacy use? Express your answer as a mixed number.\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine how much flour Stacy will use, we first need to convert the mixed number \\(3\\frac{4}{5}\\) into an improper fraction. The mixed number \\(3\\frac{4}{5}\\) means \\(3 + \\frac{4}{5}\\). To convert this to an improper fraction, we multiply the whole number part by the denominator of the fractional part, and then add the numerator of the fractional part. This gives us:\n\n\\[3 \\cdot 5 + 4 = 15 + 4 = 19\\]\n\nSo, the improper fraction equivalent to \\(3\\frac{4}{5}\\) is \\(\\frac{19}{5}\\).\n\nNext, Stacy wants to use half as much flour as the recipe calls for. Therefore, we need to multiply \\(\\frac{19}{5}\\) by \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\):\n\n\\[\\frac{19}{5} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{19 \\cdot 1}{5 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{19}{10}\\]\n\nNow, we need to convert the improper fraction \\(\\frac{19}{10}\\) back into a mixed number. We do this by dividing 19 by 10. The quotient is 1 and the remainder is 9, so \\(\\frac{19}{10} = 1\\frac{9}{10}\\).\n\nThus, the amount of flour Stacy will use is \\(\\boxed{1\\frac{9}{10}}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. A recipe calls for $3\\frac{4}{5}$ cups of flour. However, Stacy wants to use half as much flour as the recipe calls for. How many cups of flour will Stacy use? Express your answer as a mixed number.\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine how many cups of flour Stacy will use, we need to find half of the amount of flour the recipe calls for. The recipe calls for \\(3\\frac{4}{5}\\) cups of flour, which can be converted to an improper fraction.\n\nFirst, convert the mixed number \\(3\\frac{4}{5}\\) to an improper fraction:\n\\[3\\frac{4}{5} = 3 + \\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{15}{5} + \\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{19}{5}.\\]\n\nNext, we need to find half of \\(\\frac{19}{5}\\):\n\\[\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{19}{5} = \\frac{19}{10}.\\]\n\nNow, convert the improper fraction \\(\\frac{19}{10}\\) back to a mixed number:\n\\[\\frac{19}{10} = 1\\frac{9}{10}.\\]\n\nTherefore, Stacy will use \\(\\boxed{1\\frac{9}{10}}\\) cups of flour."}]
