t a model of ZFC in which the continuum is larger than \\(\\aleph_1\\) (i.e., \\(2^{\\aleph_0} > \\aleph_1\\)), and in which all subsets of \\(\\mathbb{R}\\) of size \\(\\aleph_1\\) have measure zero. This is known as the Solovay model, although the specific construction in this case is more complex than the original Solovay model, which requires the Axiom of Determinacy to hold.\n\nIn summary, it is consistent with ZFC that all subsets of \\(\\mathbb{R}\\) with cardinality \\(\\aleph_1\\) have Lebesgue measure zero, without assuming the Continuum Hypothesis. Therefore, the answer is:\n\n\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Is it consistent with Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory with the Axiom of Choice (ZFC) that all subsets of the real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\) with cardinality \\( \\aleph_1 \\) have Lebesgue measure zero, without assuming the Continuum Hypothesis (CH)?\nassistant\n", "response": "The statement \"all subsets of the real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\) with cardinality \\( \\aleph_1 \\) have Lebesgue measure zero\" is consistent with ZFC, even without assuming the Continuum Hypothesis (CH). This can be shown through the construction of a model of ZFC where this statement holds true.\n\nHere is the reasoning step by step:\n\n1. **Understanding the Statement**: The statement in question is that any subset of \\( \\mathbb{R} \\) with cardinality \\( \\aleph_1 \\) has Lebesgue measure zero. This means that even though the subset has a non-countable number of elements, its \"size\" in terms of length, area, or volume is zero.\n\n2. **Consistency with ZFC**: The consistency of this statement with ZFC can be demonstrated through the use of forcing, a method in set theory developed by Paul Cohen. Specifically, it is possible to construct a model of ZFC where the Continuum Hypothesis (CH) is false (i.e., \\( 2^{\\aleph_0} > \\aleph_1 \\