\n\n1. **Understanding the Problem:**\n   - There are 10 people seated at the vertices of a regular decagon.\n   - Some of them are liars (who always lie) and some are knights (who always tell the truth).\n   - The traveler can ask each person the question: \"What is the distance from me to the nearest liar among you?\"\n   - We need to find the minimum number of questions the traveler must ask to guarantee identifying all the liars.\n\n2. **Analyzing the Problem:**\n   - If the traveler asks a knight, the knight will truthfully report the distance to the nearest liar.\n   - If the traveler asks a liar, the liar will lie about the distance to the nearest liar.\n   - The key is to find a strategy that allows the traveler to distinguish between knights and liars by asking the minimum number of questions.\n\n3. **Strategy:**\n   - The traveler can ask each person the question and observe the responses.\n   - If the traveler asks a knight, the knight will report a specific distance.\n   - If the traveler asks a liar, the liar will report a different distance.\n   - By comparing the responses, the traveler can identify the liars.\n\n4. **Minimum Number of Questions:**\n   - The traveler needs to ask enough questions to distinguish between the knights and liars.\n   - If the traveler asks each person individually, the traveler will have the responses from all 10 people.\n   - By comparing the responses, the traveler can identify the liars.\n   - Therefore, the traveler needs to ask each of the 10 people the question.\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - The minimum number of questions the traveler must ask to guarantee identifying all the liars is 10.\n\nThus, the minimum number of questions the traveler must ask is \\(\\boxed{10}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. In a land of knights (who always tell the truth) and liars (who always lie), 10 people are seated at th