rac{x}{1+x}\\), so we substitute \\(y\\) into the binomial series expansion:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty} \\binom{-p-1}{j} \\left( -\\frac{x}{1+x} \\right)^j = \\left( 1 - \\left( -\\frac{x}{1+x} \\right) \\right)^{-p-1} = \\left( 1 + \\frac{x}{1+x} \\right)^{-p-1}\n\\]\nSimplifying the expression inside the parentheses, we get:\n\\[\n1 + \\frac{x}{1+x} = \\frac{1+x+x}{1+x} = \\frac{1+2x}{1+x}\n\\]\nThus, the series evaluates to:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1+2x}{1+x} \\right)^{-p-1} = \\left( \\frac{1+x}{1+2x} \\right)^{p+1}\n\\]\nTherefore, the value of the infinite series is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left( \\frac{1+x}{1+2x} \\right)^{p+1}}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the infinite series \\( \\sum _{j=0}^{\\infty }\\binom{-p-1}{j} \\left( -\\frac {x} {1+x}\\right) ^{j} \\) in terms of \\( p \\) and \\( x \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the infinite series \\(\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty} \\binom{-p-1}{j} \\left( -\\frac{x}{1+x} \\right)^j\\), we recognize that this is a binomial series. The binomial series expansion for \\((1+z)^a\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n(1+z)^a = \\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty} \\binom{a}{j} z^j\n\\]\n\nIn our case, \\(a = -p-1\\) and \\(z = -\\frac{x}{1+x}\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the series as:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty} \\binom{-p-1}{j} \\left( -\\frac{x}{1+x} \\right)^j = \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{1+x}\\right)^{-p-1}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{x}{1+x} = \\frac{1+x}{1+x} - \\frac{x}{1+x} = \\frac{1+x-x}{1+x} = \\frac{1}{1+x}\n\\]\n\nThus, the series becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1}{1+x} \\right)^{-p-1} = (1+x)^{p+1}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the infinite series is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(1+x)^{p+1}}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the infinite ser