 because between any two real numbers, there exists a rational number.\n\n2. **Check the measure of the set**: The set of rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) has measure zero. This is because the rational numbers can be listed in a sequence, and the measure of a countable set of points is zero.\n\n3. **Verify the closure of the set**: The closure of a set \\( A \\), denoted \\( \\bar{A} \\), is the smallest closed set containing \\( A \\). Since \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is dense in \\( \\mathbb{R} \\), the closure of \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is \\( \\mathbb{R} \\). This is because every real number is either a rational number or a limit point of the rational numbers.\n\nTherefore, the set \\( A = \\mathbb{Q} \\) is a set with measure zero such that its closure is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathbb{Q}}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Provide an example of a set \\( A \\) with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\), denoted \\( \\bar{A} \\), is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To find a set \\( A \\) with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\), denoted \\( \\bar{A} \\), is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\), we can consider the set of rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\).\n\n1. **Measure Zero**: The set of rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is countable. Since a countable set has measure zero, the set \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) has measure zero.\n2. **Closure is \\( \\mathbb{R} \\)**: The closure of a set is the smallest closed set containing that set. The closure of \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is the set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\), because every real number is either a rational number or a limit point of the rational numbers.\n\nTherefore, the set \\( A = \\mathbb{Q} \\) satisfies the conditions of the problem.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\mathbb{Q}}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Provide an example of a set \\( A \\) with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\), denoted \\( \\bar{A} \\), is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To find a set \\( A \\) with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\), denoted \\( \\bar{A} \\), is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\), we can consider the set of all rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\).\n\nHere is the reasoning step by step:\n\n1. **Measure Zero**: The set of all rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is countable. A countable set has measure zero. Therefore, the measure of \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is zero.\n\n2. **Closure**: The closure of a set is the smallest closed set containing the set. The closure of the set of all rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is the set of all real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\). This is because every real number can be approximated arbitrarily closely by rational numbers. In other words, the set of all rational numbers is dense in the set of all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the set \\( A = \\mathbb{Q} \\) is a set with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\) is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\mathbb{Q}}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Provide an example of a set \\( A \\) with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\), denoted \\( \\bar{A} \\), is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To find a set \\( A \\) with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\), denoted \\( \\bar{A} \\), is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\), we need to consider a set that is dense in \\( \\mathbb{R} \\) but has measure zero. A classic example of such a set is the set of all rational numbers, denoted \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\).\n\nThe set of rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is dense in \\( \\mathbb{R} \\), meaning that every real number can be approximated arbitrarily closely by rational numbers. This implies that the closure of \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is \\( \\mathbb{R} \\). Additionally, the set of rational numbers is countable, and hence has measure zero.\n\nTherefore, the set \\( A = \\mathbb{Q} \\) is a set with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\) is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathbb{Q}}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Provide an example of a set \\( A \\) with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\), denoted \\( \\bar{A} \\), is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To find a set \\( A \\) with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\), denoted \\( \\bar{A} \\), is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\), we can consider the set of all rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\).\n\nThe set of rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) has measure zero because it is a countable set. The measure of any countable set is zero. On the other hand, the closure of the set of rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\) because every real number can be approximated arbitrarily closely by a rational number.\n\nTherefore, the set \\( A \\) that satisfies the given conditions is the set of all rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\mathbb{Q}}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Provide an example of a set \\( A \\) with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\), denoted \\( \\bar{A} \\), is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To find a set \\( A \\) with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\), denoted \\( \\bar{A} \\), is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\), we can consider the set of all rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\).\n\nThe set of rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is countable, and the measure of any countable set is zero. Therefore, the measure of \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is zero.\n\nThe closure of \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is the smallest closed set containing all rational numbers. Since every real number can be approximated arbitrarily closely by rational numbers, the closure of \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is the set of all real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\).\n\nThus, the set \\( A = \\mathbb{Q} \\) satisfies the conditions of the problem. Therefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathbb{Q}}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Provide an example of a set \\( A \\) with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\), denoted \\( \\bar{A} \\), is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To find a set \\( A \\) with measure zero such that the closure of \\( A \\), denoted \\( \\bar{A} \\), is equal to the entire set of real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\), we can consider the set of all rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\).\n\n1. The set of all rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is countable. Since any countable set has measure zero, the set \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) has measure zero.\n2. The closure of the set of all rational numbers \\( \\mathbb{Q} \\) is the set of all real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\). This is because every real number can be approximated arbitrarily closely by a sequence of rational numbers.\n\nTherefore, the set \\( A = \\mathbb{Q} \\) satisfies the conditions of the problem.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\mathbb{Q}}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. A new green technology can be installed in your home for a one-time installation cost of $\\$10,\\!000$ and annual maintenance costs of $\\$650$ per year. If the green technology is supposed to save you $\\$1200$ each year in energy bills, how many whole years will it take to recuperate your costs?\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine how many whole years it will take to recuperate the costs of the green technology, we need to calculate the net savings each year and then find out how many years it will take for the total net savings to equal or exceed the initial installation and maintenance costs.\n\n1. **Calculate the initial costs:**\n   - One-time installation cost: \\$10,000\n   - Annual maintenance cost: \\$650\n   - Annual savings: \\$1200\n\n2. **Calculate the net savings per year:**\n   - Net savings per year = Annual savings - Annual maintenance cost\n   - Net savings per year = \\$1200 - \\$650 = \\$550\n\n3. **Determine the number of years required to recuperate the initial costs:**\n   - We need to find the number of years \\( n \\) such that the total net savings over \\( n \\) years equals or exceeds the initial installation cost.\n   - Total net savings over \\( n \\) years = Net savings per year \\(\\times n\\)\n   - Total net savings over \\( n \\) years = \\$550 \\(\\times n\\)\n   - We need \\( 550 \\times n \\geq 10000 \\)\n\n4. **Solve for \\( n \\):**\n   - \\( n \\geq \\frac{10000}{550} \\)\n   - \\( n \\geq 18.18 \\)\n\nSince \\( n \\) must be a whole number, we round up to the next whole number, which is 19.\n\nTherefore, it will take \\(\\boxed{19}\\) whole years to recuperate the costs."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. A new green technology can be installed in your home for a one-time installation cost of $\\$10,\\!000$ and annual maintenance costs of $\\$650$ per year. If the green technology is supposed to save you $\\$1200$ each year in energy bills, how many whole years will it take to recuperate your costs?\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine how many whole years it will take to recuperate the costs of the green technology, we need to calculate the net savings each year and then find out how many years it will take for the net savings to equal or exceed the initial installation cost plus the cumulative maintenance costs.\n\n1. The initial installation cost is $10,000.\n2. The annual maintenance cost is $650.\n3. The annual energy savings are $1,200.\n\nThe net savings each year is the annual energy savings minus the annual maintenance cost:\n\\[ \\text{Net savings per year} = \\$1,200 - \\$650 = \\$550 \\]\n\nWe need to find the number of years \\( n \\) such that the total net savings equal or exceed the initial installation cost:\n\\[ 550n \\geq 10,000 \\]\n\\[ n \\geq \\frac{10,000}{550} \\]\n\\[ n \\geq 18.18 \\]\n\nSince we are looking for the number of whole years, we round up to the next whole number:\n\\[ n = 19 \\]\n\nTherefore, it will take \\(\\boxed{19}\\) whole years to recuperate the costs."}]
