[2026-04-08 12:43:13] [rlvr_pipeline.py (574)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1109663] [{"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Does there exist a graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013, where all cycles have a length greater than 2013?\nassistant\n", "response": "\\[\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}.\\]\n\nTo construct such a graph, we can use a Ramanujan graph. A Ramanujan graph is a type of expander graph that has a high chromatic number and can be constructed with a girth (the length of the shortest cycle) that is greater than any given number.\n\nHere's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Definition of Ramanujan Graph**: A Ramanujan graph is a regular graph that is an optimal expander. It has a high chromatic number and can be constructed with a girth greater than any given number.\n\n2. **Girth and Cycles**: The girth of a graph is the length of the shortest cycle in the graph. If a graph has a girth greater than 2013, then all cycles in the graph have a length greater than 2013.\n\n3. **Chromatic Number**: The chromatic number of a graph is the smallest number of colors needed to color the vertices of the graph such that no two adjacent vertices share the same color. Ramanujan graphs can be constructed to have a chromatic number greater than any given number.\n\n4. **Construction**: By construction, Ramanujan graphs can have a chromatic number greater than 2013 and a girth greater than 2013. Therefore, there exists a graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013 where all cycles have a length greater than 2013.\n\nThus, the answer is \\[\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}.\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Does there exist a graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013, where all cycles have a length greater than 2013?\nassistant\n", "response": "\\[\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}.\\]\n\nTo construct such a graph, we can use the concept of the Mycielskian, which is a method for constructing triangle-free graphs with arbitrarily high chromatic numbers. The Mycielskian of a graph \\(G\\) is a new graph \\(M(G)\\) that has a chromatic number one more than that of \\(G\\), and it is triangle-free if \\(G\\) is triangle-free.\n\nWe start with a triangle-free graph with a chromatic number of 3, such as the 5-cycle (which has a chromatic number of 3 and all cycles have length 5). Then, we repeatedly apply the Mycielskian operation. Each application increases the chromatic number by 1 and preserves the property that all cycles have a length of at least 5.\n\nAfter applying the Mycielskian operation 2010 times to the 5-cycle, we obtain a graph with a chromatic number of \\(3 + 2010 = 2013\\), and all cycles have a length of at least 5. To obtain a graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013, we apply the Mycielskian operation one more time. This results in a graph with a chromatic number of 2014, and all cycles have a length of at least 5.\n\nTherefore, there exists a graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013, where all cycles have a length greater than 2013.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Does there exist a graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013, where all cycles have a length greater than 2013?\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{\\text{Yes.}}. We can construct a graph by starting with a complete graph on 2014 vertices and then adding a new vertex for each edge in the complete graph, connecting it to the endpoints of that edge. This graph has a chromatic number of 2014 (since the original complete graph on 2014 vertices requires 2014 colors) and all cycles have a length greater than 2013 (since the only cycles are those formed by the new vertices, and each new vertex is only connected to two vertices in the original complete graph)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Does there exist a graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013, where all cycles have a length greater than 2013?\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}.\n\nTo construct such a graph, we can use the Mycielski construction, which is a method to construct triangle-free graphs with arbitrarily high chromatic number. The Mycielski construction starts with a graph \\( G_1 \\) (in this case, a single vertex), and iteratively constructs a new graph \\( G_{k+1} \\) from \\( G_k \\).\n\nThe chromatic number of \\( G_k \\) is \\( k \\). We can continue this process until we reach a graph \\( G_{2014} \\), which will have a chromatic number of 2014. This graph will be triangle-free, and hence all cycles will have a length greater than 2013.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Does there exist a graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013, where all cycles have a length greater than 2013?\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}.\n\nA graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013 where all cycles have a length greater than 2013 can be constructed by considering a complete graph \\( K_{2014} \\) and removing a perfect matching from it. The chromatic number of the resulting graph is still 2014 because we have removed only a perfect matching, which does not affect the chromatic number. The resulting graph has no cycles of length less than or equal to 2013 because the shortest cycle in a complete graph \\( K_n \\) with a perfect matching removed is of length \\( n-1 \\), which in this case is 2013. Therefore, the graph has a chromatic number greater than 2013 and all cycles have a length greater than 2013.\n\nThe answer is \\boxed{\\text{Yes}}."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Does there exist a graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013, where all cycles have a length greater than 2013?\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}. Consider a complete graph \\(K_n\\) with \\(n > 2013\\). The chromatic number of \\(K_n\\) is \\(n\\), which is greater than 2013. In \\(K_n\\), every set of 3 vertices forms a cycle of length 3, which is greater than 2013. Therefore, there exists a graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013, where all cycles have a length greater than 2013.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Does there exist a graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013, where all cycles have a length greater than 2013?\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}.\n\nTo construct such a graph, consider the following:\n\n1. Start with a cycle of length 2014. This cycle has a chromatic number of 2 because it is an even cycle.\n2. Add a new vertex and connect it to every vertex of the cycle. This new graph is a wheel graph with 2015 vertices.\n3. The chromatic number of this wheel graph is 3 because the hub vertex can be colored with a third color.\n4. To increase the chromatic number, we can continue adding vertices and connecting them to every vertex of the previous cycle, forming a larger wheel graph.\n5. By continuing this process, we can construct a graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013, where all cycles have a length greater than 2013.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\boxed{\\text{Yes}}."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Does there exist a graph with a chromatic number greater than 2013, where all cycles have a length greater than 2013?\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}. Consider a complete graph $G$ with 2014 vertices. The chromatic number of $G$ is 2014, which is greater than 2013. Also, all cycles in $G$ have a length of at least 3, which is greater than 2013. Therefore, such a graph exists.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the limit: $$\\lim_{(x,y)\\to(0,0)} \\frac{xy(x^2-y^2)}{x^2 + y^2}$$\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the limit \\(\\lim_{(x,y)\\to(0,0)} \\frac{xy(x^2-y^2)}{x^2 + y^2}\\), we can use polar coordinates. Let \\(x = r\\cos\\theta\\) and \\(y = r\\sin\\theta\\). Then, as \\((x,y) \\to (0,0)\\), we have \\(r \\to 0\\).\n\nSubstituting \\(x = r\\cos\\theta\\) and \\(y = r\\sin\\theta\\) into the expression, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{xy(x^2-y^2)}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{(r\\cos\\theta)(r\\sin\\theta)((r\\cos\\theta)^2 - (r\\sin\\theta)^2)}{r^2} = \\frac{r^2 \\cos\\theta \\sin\\theta (r^2 (\\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta))}{r^2} = r^3 \\cos\\theta \\sin\\theta (\\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta).\n\\]\n\nAs \\(r \\to 0\\), the expression \\(r^3 \\cos\\theta \\sin\\theta (\\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta) \\to 0\\) because \\(r^3 \\to 0\\) and \\(\\cos\\theta \\sin\\theta (\\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta)\\) is bounded. Therefore, the limit is:\n\\[\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to(0,0)} \\frac{xy(x^2-y^2)}{x^2 + y^2} = \\boxed{0}.\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the limit: $$\\lim_{(x,y)\\to(0,0)} \\frac{xy(x^2-y^2)}{x^2 + y^2}$$\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the limit \\(\\lim_{(x,y)\\to(0,0)} \\frac{xy(x^2-y^2)}{x^2 + y^2}\\), we can use polar coordinates. Let \\(x = r \\cos \\theta\\) and \\(y = r \\sin \\theta\\). Then, as \\((x, y) \\to (0, 0)\\), we have \\(r \\to 0\\).\n\nSubstitute \\(x = r \\cos \\theta\\) and \\(y = r \\sin \\theta\\) into the expression:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{xy(x^2 - y^2)}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{(r \\cos \\theta)(r \\sin \\theta)((r \\cos \\theta)^2 - (r \\sin \\theta)^2)}{(r \\cos \\theta)^2 + (r \\sin \\theta)^2}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{r^2 \\cos \\theta \\sin \\theta (r^2 \\cos^2 \\theta - r^2 \\sin^2 \\theta)}{r^2 (\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta)} = \\frac{r^2 \\cos \\theta \\sin \\theta r^2 (\\cos^2 \\theta - \\sin^2 \\theta)}{r^2} = r^2 \\cos \\theta \\sin \\theta (\\cos^2 \\theta - \\sin^2 \\theta)\n\\]\n\nAs \\(r \\to 0\\), the expression \\(r^2 \\cos \\theta \\sin \\theta (\\cos^2 \\theta - \\sin^2 \\theta) \\to 0\\), regardless of the value of \\(\\theta\\).\n\nTherefore, the limit is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]"}]
