ext{Yes}}."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Can a CW complex have a 2-dimensional cell without any 1-dimensional cells, such that the 1-skeleton is equal to the 0-skeleton?\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}. A CW complex can indeed have a 2-dimensional cell without any 1-dimensional cells, such that the 1-skeleton is equal to the 0-skeleton. This is possible if the attachment map of the 2-cell is a constant map, which maps the entire boundary of the 2-cell to a single point in the 0-skeleton. In this case, the 1-skeleton is just the 0-skeleton, and the 2-cell is attached to a single point in the 0-skeleton.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\boxed{\\text{Yes}}."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Let \\[f(x) =\n\\begin{cases}\n3x^2 + 2&\\text{if } x\\le 3, \\\\\nax - 1 &\\text{if } x>3.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]Find $a$ if the graph of $y=f(x)$ is continuous (which means the graph can be drawn without lifting your pencil from the paper).\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) that makes the function \\(f(x)\\) continuous at \\(x=3\\), we need to ensure that the left-hand limit and the right-hand limit of \\(f(x)\\) at \\(x=3\\) are equal to the value of the function at \\(x=3\\).\n\nThe function \\(f(x)\\) is defined as:\n\\[f(x) =\n\\begin{cases}\n3x^2 + 2 & \\text{if } x \\le 3, \\\\\nax - 1 & \\text{if } x > 3.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we find the value of the function at \\(x=3\\):\n\\[f(3) = 3(3)^2 + 2 = 3 \\cdot 9 + 2 = 27 + 2 = 29.\\]\n\nNext, we find the right-hand limit of \\(f(x)\\) as \\(x\\) approaches 3 from the right:\n\\[\\lim_{x \\to 3^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 3^+} (ax - 1) = 3a - 1.\\]\n\nFor the function to be continuous at \\(x=3\\), the right-hand limit must equal the value of the function at \\(x=3\\):\n\\[3a - 1 = 29.\\]\n\nSolving for \\(a\\):\n\\[3a - 1 = 29,\\]\n\\[3a = 30,\\]\n\\[a = 10.\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a\\) that makes the function \\(f(x)\\) continuous is \\(\\boxed{10}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Let \\[f(x) =\n\\begin{cases}\n3x^2 + 2&\\text{if } x\\le 3, \\\\\nax - 1 &\\text{if } x>3.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]Find $a$ if the graph of $y=f(x)$ is continuous (which means the graph can be drawn without lifting your pencil from the paper).\nassistant\n", "response": "To find the value of \\(a\\) that makes the function \\(f(x)\\) continuous, we need to ensure that the function is continuous at the point where the definition changes, which is at \\(x = 3\\). This means that the left-hand limit of \\(f(x)\\) as \\(x\\) approaches 3 must be equal to the right-hand limit of \\(f(x)\\) as \\(x\\) approaches 3, and both must be equal to the value of the function at \\(x = 3\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of the function at \\(x = 3\\):\n\\[ f(3) = 3(3)^2 + 2 = 3 \\cdot 9 + 2 = 27 + 2 = 29. \\]\n\nNext, we find the left-hand limit of \\(f(x)\\) as \\(x\\) approaches 3:\n\\[ \\lim_{x \\to 3^-} f(x) = 3(3)^2 + 2 = 29. \\]\n\nThen, we find the right-hand limit of \\(f(x)\\) as \\(x\\) approaches 3:\n\\[ \\lim_{x \\to 3^+} f(x) = a(3) - 1 = 3a - 1. \\]\n\nFor the function to be continuous at \\(x = 3\\), the left-hand limit must equal the right-hand limit and both must equal the value of the function at \\(x = 3\\):\n\\[ 29 = 3a - 1. \\]\n\nNow, we solve for \\(a\\):\n\\[ 29 = 3a - 1 \\]\n\\[ 29 + 1 = 3a \\]\n\\[ 30 = 3a \\]\n\\[ a = 10. \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a\\) that makes the function \\(f(x)\\) continuous is \\(\\boxed{10}\\)."}]
