 the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{n!}}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Let $X$ be a smooth, rationally connected variety over $\\mathbb{C}$. Given two points $p,q\\in X$, determine if there always exists a smooth rational curve $C$ containing both $p$ and $q$. Provide a justification for your answer.\nassistant\n", "response": "Given that \\(X\\) is a smooth, rationally connected variety over \\(\\mathbb{C}\\), by definition, for any two points \\(p, q \\in X\\), there exists a rational curve (i.e., a curve isomorphic to \\(\\mathbb{P}^1\\)) that passes through both \\(p\\) and \\(q\\). However, the rational curve may not be smooth. \n\nTo determine if there always exists a smooth rational curve \\(C\\) containing both \\(p\\) and \\(q\\), we need to consider the properties of rational curves in rationally connected varieties. \n\nA key result in algebraic geometry is that on a rationally connected variety, one can find a smooth rational curve passing through any two points. This is a consequence of the fact that rational curves in rationally connected varieties can be deformed to avoid singularities and self-intersections.\n\nTherefore, the answer is that there always exists a smooth rational curve \\(C\\) containing both \\(p\\) and \\(q\\).\n\n\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Let $X$ be a smooth, rationally connected variety over $\\mathbb{C}$. Given two points $p,q\\in X$, determine if there always exists a smooth rational curve $C$ containing both $p$ and $q$. Provide a justification for your answer.\nassistant\n", "response": "\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}. Let \\( X \\) be a smooth, rationally connected variety over \\(\\mathbb{C}\\). By definition, a variety \\( X \\) is rationally connected if for any 