( \\Lambda(\\gamma) = \\lim_{d \\to b} \\int_a^d |\\gamma'(t)| \\, dt \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine whether the statement is true or false, we need to analyze the given expression for the length of the curve \\(\\gamma\\).\n\nThe length of a curve \\(\\gamma(t)\\) that is continuously differentiable on \\([a, b)\\) is given by the integral of the magnitude of the derivative of \\(\\gamma(t)\\) over the interval \\([a, b)\\). This can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\Lambda(\\gamma) = \\int_a^b |\\gamma'(t)| \\, dt\n\\]\n\nHowever, since the curve is only continuously differentiable on \\([a, b)\\) and not necessarily at \\(b\\), the integral is an improper integral. To handle this, we can take the limit as \\(d\\) approaches \\(b\\) from the left:\n\n\\[\n\\Lambda(\\gamma) = \\lim_{d \\to b^-} \\int_a^d |\\gamma'(t)| \\, dt\n\\]\n\nThis expression is exactly the same as the one given in the statement. Therefore, the statement is true.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{true}}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Given a rectangle with sides $a$ and $b$, and an inscribed square with side length $l$, determine the minimum number of additional squares required to fill the remaining area of the rectangle, assuming that the squares cannot be of the same size.\nassistant\n", "response": "Given a rectangle with sides \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), and an inscribed square with side length \\(l\\), we need to determine the minimum number of additional squares required to fill the remaining area of the rectangle, assuming that the squares cannot be of the same size.\n\nFirst, let's consider the inscribed square with side length \\(l\\). The square will fit into the rectangle in such a way that either \\(l = a\\) or \\(l = b\\), depending on which side is shorter. Without loss of generality, let's assume \\(a \\leq b\\), so the inscribed square will have side length \\(a\\).\n\nAfter placing the square of side length \\(a\\), the remaining area of the rectangle is a smaller rectangle with dimensions \\(a\\) and \\(b-a\\). We need to fill this remaining area with additional squares of different sizes.\n\nThe simplest way to fill the remaining area is to use a square of side length \\(b-a\\) and a smaller square of side length \\(a - (b-a) = 2a - b\\) (if \\(2a > b\\)), and continue this process until the entire area is filled. However, since the problem specifies that the squares cannot be of the same size, we need to consider the minimum number of squares required.\n\nThe minimum number of additional squares required to fill the remaining area is 1, because we can always fill the remaining area with a single square of side length \\(b-a\\) if \\(b-a < a\\), or with two squares if \\(b-a \\geq a\\).\n\nTherefore, the minimum number of additional squares required to fill the remaining area of the rectangle is \\(\\boxed{2}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Given a rectangle with sides $a$ and $b$, and an inscribed square with side length $l$, determine the minimum number of additional squares required to fill the remaining area of the rectangle, assuming that the squares cannot be of the same size.\nassistant\n", "response": "Given a rectangle with sides \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) and an inscribed square with side length \\(l\\), we need to determine the minimum number of additional squares required to fill the remaining area of the rectangle, assuming that the squares cannot be of the same size.\n\nFirst, let's assume without loss of generality that \\(a \\geq b\\). The side length of the inscribed square is \\(b\\), so the remaining area of the rectangle is a smaller rectangle with dimensions \\(a - b\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nNext, we need to fill this smaller rectangle with squares of different sizes. The largest possible square we can fit in this smaller rectangle is a square with side length \\(a - b\\). After placing this square, the remaining area is a smaller rectangle with dimensions \\(a - b\\) and \\(b - (a - b) = 2b - a\\).\n\nWe continue this process, fitting the largest possible square in the remaining area each time. The process will continue until the remaining area is a square or a degenerate rectangle (a line segment).\n\nThe number of additional squares required is the number of steps in this process. Each step reduces the dimensions of the remaining rectangle, and the process stops when the remaining area is a square or a degenerate rectangle.\n\nThe minimum number of additional squares required to fill the remaining area of the rectangle is \\(\\boxed{1}\\). This is because after placing the largest possible square in the remaining area, the remaining area is either a square or a degenerate rectangle, which does not require any additional squares to fill.\n\nTherefore, the minimum number of additional squares required to fill the remaining area of the rectangle is \\(\\boxed{1}\\)."}]
