are already counted. Therefore, there are \\(12 \\times (3-2) = 12\\) edge cubes, and each edge cube has 2 faces painted red.\n\nAdding these together, we get:\n\\[8 \\text{ (corner cubes)} + 12 \\text{ (edge cubes)} = 20\\]\n\nThus, the number of one-inch cubes that have red paint on at least two faces is \\(\\boxed{20}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. The six faces of a three-inch wooden cube are each painted red. The cube is then cut into one-inch cubes along the lines shown in the diagram. How many of the one-inch cubes have red paint on at least two faces? [asy]\n\npair A,B,C,D,E,F,G;\n\npair a,c,d,f,g,i,j,l,m,o,p,r,s,u,v,x,b,h;\n\nA=(0.8,1);\nB=(0,1.2);\nC=(1.6,1.3);\nD=(0.8,0);\nE=B-(A-D);\nF=C-(A-D);\nG=B+(C-A);\n\ndraw(E--D--F--C--G--B--A--D);\ndraw(A--C); draw(E--B);\n\na=(1/3)*D+(2/3)*E;\nc=(2/3)*D+(1/3)*E;\np=(1/3)*A+(2/3)*B;\nr=(2/3)*A+(1/3)*B;\n\ndraw(a--p);\ndraw(c--r);\n\nv=(1/3)*B+(2/3)*E;\nx=(2/3)*B+(1/3)*E;\nb=(1/3)*A+(2/3)*D;\nh=(2/3)*A+(1/3)*D;\n\ndraw(v--b);\ndraw(x--h);\n\ns=(1/3)*C+(2/3)*A;\nu=(2/3)*C+(1/3)*A;\nd=(1/3)*F+(2/3)*D;\nf=(2/3)*F+(1/3)*D;\n\ndraw(s--d);\ndraw(f--u);\n\ng=(1/3)*C+(2/3)*F;\ni=(2/3)*C+(1/3)*F;\n\ndraw(i--h);\ndraw(g--b);\n\nm=(1/3)*B+(2/3)*G;\no=(2/3)*B+(1/3)*G;\n\ndraw(m--u);\ndraw(o--s);\n\nj=(1/3)*G+(2/3)*C;\nl=(2/3)*G+(1/3)*C;\n\ndraw(l--p);\ndraw(r--j);\n\n[/asy]\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine how many of the one-inch cubes have red paint on at least two faces, we need to analyze the structure of the original three-inch cube and how it is cut into smaller cubes.\n\n1. **Identify the structure of the original cube:**\n   - The original cube is a 3x3x3 cube, which means it is cut into \\(3^3 = 27\\) smaller one-inch cubes.\n\n2. **Categorize the smaller cubes based on the number of faces painted:**\n   - **Cubes with 3 faces painted:** These are the corner cubes. A 3x3x3 cube has 8 corners.\n   - **Cubes with 2 faces painted:** These are the edge cubes, excluding the corners. Each edge of the cube has 3 cubes, but the two at the ends are corners, so each edge has 3-2 = 1 cube with 2 faces painted. Since a cube has 12 edges, there are \\(12 \\times 1 = 12\\) cubes with 2 faces painted.\n   - **Cubes with 1 face painted:** These are the face cubes, excluding the edges and corners. Each face of the cube is a 3x3 grid, but the outermost cubes are either edges or corners, so each face has a 1x1 grid in the center with 1 cube. Since a cube has 6 faces, there are \\(6 \\times 1 = 6\\) cubes with 1 face painted.\n   - **Cubes with 0 faces painted:** These are the interior cubes. A 3x3x3 cube has only one interior cube.\n\n3. **Count the cubes with at least 2 faces painted:**\n   - Cubes with 3 faces painted: 8\n   - Cubes with 2 faces painted: 12\n   - Total cubes with at least 2 faces painted: \\(8 + 12 = 20\\)\n\nTherefore, the number of one-inch cubes that have red paint on at least two faces is \\(\\boxed{20}\\)."}]
